'''''Ornithogalum umbellatum''''', the '''garden star-of-Bethlehem''', '''grass lily''', '''nap-at-noon''', or '''eleven-o'clock lady''', is a [[perennial]] [[bulbous]] [[flowering plant]] in the asparagus family ([[Asparagaceae]]). ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' is a relatively short plant, occurring in tufts of basal linear leaves, producing conspicuous white flowers, in a stellate pattern, in mid to late spring. The flowers open late in the day (hence some of its common names), but when closed have a green stripe on the outside. It is [[native species|native]] throughout southern and central [[Europe]], and north-western [[Africa]]. ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' is often grown as a [[garden ornamental]], but in [[North America]] and other areas it has escaped cultivation and can be found in many areas, where it may be considered an [[Invasive species|invasive]] weed. Parts of the plant are considered poisonous, but are used in some regional cuisines. [[Essential oil|Essences]] are also sold as [[patent remedies]]. ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' has been depicted in art by artists such as [[Leonardo da Vinci]], and [[folklore]] has suggested it originally grew from fragments of the [[star of Bethlehem]], hence its horticultural name.

==Description==
{{See also|Floral diagram}}
''O. umbellatum'' is a [[perennial]] [[herbaceous]] [[bulbous]] plant ([[geophyte]]), dying back after flowering, to an underground storage bulb. The following year, it regrows from the often shallow rooted bulbs, which are [[ovoid]] with a membranous coat,{{sfn |IPANE |2019}} {{convert|15|-|25|mm|abbr=off|frac=4}} long and {{convert|18|-|32|mm|abbr=on|frac=4}} in diameter. The bulbs form multiple leaf-bearing bulbils that soon separate but remain close by.{{sfn |Gadella |1972}} Initially the plant forms 6–10 basal [[leaf|leaves]], that arise in tufts from the bulbs. The leaves are grooved ([[Glossary of botanical terms#canaliculate|canaliculate]]), smooth ([[glabrousness (botany)|glabrous]]) and linear with a white to light green linear midrib on the upper surface, and grow up to {{convert|30|cm}} long and {{convert|8|mm|1|abbr=on|frac=4}} broad.{{sfn |Tutin et al |1980 |p=39}}

''O. umbellatum'' is [[scapose]], with a glabrous flower stem ([[Scape (botany)|scape]]) that emerges from the leaf tufts later and is about {{convert|10|-|30|cm|0|abbr=on}} in height, tapering at its tip.{{sfn |IPANE |2019}} 
