==Taxonomy==
{{multiple image | header = | align = center | direction = horizontal | total_width= 250 | float = none 
|image1 =Star of Bethlem.png|caption1=Illustration by [[John Gerard]]<br>1597|alt1=John Gerard's illustration
|image2 =Ornithogalum.png|caption2=Description by [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]]<br>1753|alt2=page from Linnaeus' Species Plantarum, describing Ornithogalum umbellatum
}}
''Ornithogalum umbellatum'' has been known and described for a long time, according to some authors, as far back as [[Dioscorides]] in the first century.{{sfn |Dioscorides |2000 |p=300}}{{sfn |Lyte |1586}}{{sfn |Grieve |1931}} In 16th-century England, [[William Turner (naturalist)|William Turner]] (1562) mentions it.{{sfn |Turner |1995 |p={{pn|date=October 2020}}}} In [[Henry Lyte (botanist)|Henry Lyte]]'s 1586 ''New Herbal'' (a translation into English of [[Dodoens]]' 1554 ''Cruydeboeck''), reference is made to an ''Ornithogalum'' as the White Field Onion. [[John Gerard]], in his ''Herball'' of 1597, describes ''Ornithogalum'' as the "star of Bethlem".{{sfn |Gerard |1597}} The species known today as ''Ornithogalum umbellatum'' was first formally described by [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] (1753),{{sfn |Linnaeus |1753}} who is the [[botanical authority]] for the [[species]] (L.). ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' is the [[type species]] of the [[genus]] ''[[Ornithogalum]]'', which contains about 50 species.{{sfn|Martinez-Azorin et al.|2011}} The [[lectotype]] (reference specimen) was established by [[W. T. Stearn|Stearn]] in 1983.{{sfn |Stearn |1983}}

===Etymology===
The species [[epithet]] reflects the flower formation as an [[umbel]].{{sfn |MBG |2019}} The plant's many common names include garden star-of-Bethlehem,{{sfn |BSBI |2019}} sleepydick,{{sfn |USDA |2019}} nap-at-noon,{{sfn |FNA |2003}} grass lily, summer snowflake, snowdrop, starflower, bird's milk, chinkerichee, ten-o'clock lady, eleven-o'clock lady, Bath asparagus, and star of Hungary. The references to the time of day reflect the opening times of the flowers, opening late and closing at night or in cloudy conditions. The star names indicate the arrangement of the petals and bird's milk is a literal translation of ornithogalum.{{sfn |Muma |2019}}{{sfn |DG |2019}}{{sfn |Van Engelen |2019}} It has also been called [[dove's dung]], equating it with a plant described in the bible.{{sfn |Balfour |1885}} {{sfn |Steckel |McClure |2015}} The name star-of-Bethlehem is shared by a number of species of ''Ornithogalum'', due to the white stellate flowers, and the folklore relating the flower to the biblical [[Star of Bethlehem|star-of-Bethlehem]] (''see [[#ipc|In popular culture]]'').{{sfn |Cotes |1898}}

==Distribution and habitat==
A native of most of Europe,{{sfn |Euro+Med Plantbase |2006}} North Africa and western Asia, ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' has been adopted as an [[ornamental garden plant]] from where it has escaped and [[Naturalised species|naturalised]] widely in North America,{{sfn |FNA |2003}} where it is considered an aggressive [[noxious weed]] of lawns, gardens and no-till agricultural land, which can be difficult to eradicate.{{sfn |Steckel |McClure |2015}} In Europe its range extends from Ireland{{sfn |Devlin |2019}} and Portugal in the west, Italy in the south, north to parts of France and east to Turkey{{sfn |IPANE |2019}}{{sfn |MBG |2019}}{{sfn |USDA |2019}} and the [[Levant]].{{sfn |Cullen et al |2011 |p=117}}

''O. umbellatum'' prefers damp habitats, being found along rivers, streams, and lower, wet areas of pastures.{{sfn |Steckel |McClure |2015}} It is tolerant to shade and soil type.{{sfn |PFAF |2012}}

==Ecology==
===Life cycle===
''Ornithogalum umbellatum'' is thermoperiodic, requiring a cold winter to complete its life cycle.{{sfn|PG|2019}} It first appears in early spring as tufts of leaves, prior to flowering ([[Glossary of botanical terms#proteranthous|proteranthous]]), which occurs in late spring (May–June), the leaves fading prior to blooming. It reproduces by its [[bulbs]], which form many offsetting [[bulbils]] that can be dispersed by water.{{sfn |IPANE |2019}} Like many bulb plants from temperate regions, a period of exposure to cold is necessary before spring growth can begin. This protects the plant from growth during winter when intense cold may damage it. Warmer spring temperatures then initiate growth from the bulb. ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' spreads aggressively in clumps by means of these offsets.{{sfn |MBG |2019}}{{sfn |Flowerinfo |2019}}

===Pollination===
The flowers are [[insect pollinated]], but may also be [[self-fertile]], forming seeds in summer (June–July).{{sfn |PFAF |2012}} [[Seed dispersal]] plays a relatively minor role in propagation, but accounts for isolated blooms.{{sfn |PG |2019}}

===Effect of light===
The petals exhibit [[photoperiodism]], often opening at noon and closing at night or on cloudy days.{{sfn |FNA |2003}}
