==Cultivation==
''Ornithogalum umbellatum'' requires considerable moisture during winter and spring, but can tolerate summer drought. It can be grown in a [[woodland garden]] as semi-shade is preferable. It is [[hardiness (plants)|hardy]] to [[hardiness zone|zone]] 5 UK,{{sfn |PFAF |2012}} 4–9 USDA, and can become [[Invasive species|invasive]]. It is produced commercially as an ornamental garden plant.{{sfn |DG |2019}} To control invasive tendencies, it is best to plant in a container or an edged area.{{sfn |Badgett |2018}} ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' may be grown as an indoor plant.{{sfn |Palomo |2018}}

==Toxicity==
The plant, especially the bulb and flowers, contains [[cardiac glycosides]], specifically [[convallatoxin]] and convalloside which are toxic to humans and livestock.{{sfn |IPANE |2019}}{{sfn |Jordan |2000}}{{sfn |FNA |2003}} Symptoms of poisoning include nausea, salivation, vomiting, diarrhea, and shortness of breath, as well as pain, burning, and swelling of lips, tongue, and throat. Prolonged contact may lead to skin irritation.{{sfn |Steckel |McClure|2015}}{{sfn |Agriculture Canada |2013}}

==Uses==
Despite the known toxicities, the plant has been described as edible for 2,000 years{{sfn |Dioscorides |2000 |p=300}}{{sfn |Linnaeus |1792 |p=287}} and incorporated into some traditional regional cuisines (e.g., Turkish){{sfn |Ozbek et al |2017}} and traditional medicine (e.g., Indian).{{sfn |Krishna et al |2019}} In Britain, the plant has long been the subject of [[herbals]] for its claimed culinary and medicinal properties.{{sfn |Gerard |1597}}{{sfn |Grieve |1931}} This includes drying and grinding the bulbs, or boiling them and baking the flowers into bread.{{sfn |Flowerinfo |2019}}{{sfn |Brown |2011}} [[essential oil|Essences]] are sold as [[patent remedies]] and for [[aromatherapy]],{{sfn |Flowerinfo |2019}} such as [[Bach flower remedies]].{{sfn |Bach |2019}}{{sfn |Huxley et al |1992 |p={{pn|date=October 2020}}}} Medical authorities advise against ingesting any part of the plant.{{sfn |WebMD |2019}}

==<span class="anchor" id="ipc"></span>In culture==
[[File:Leonardo botanical study.JPG|thumb|alt=Drawing of flowers by Da Vinci|Leonardo da Vinci drawing of flowers]]
[[Leonardo da Vinci]] drew ''O.&nbsp;umbellatum'' and included the plant in one of his depictions of [[Leda and the Swan (Leonardo)|Leda and the Swan]] (1508–1515), in which the flowers are held in Leda's left hand.{{sfn |Capra |2013 |p=101}}{{sfn |RCT |2019}} In [[folklore]], the biblical star of Bethlehem is said to have fallen to the earth and shattered into pieces which became the ubiquitous white flowers.{{sfn |Cotes |1898}} Legend has also associated it with the journeys of [[Crusaders]] and [[pilgrims]] to the [[Holy Land]].{{sfn |Brown |2011}}

A biblical passage in ''2 [[Books of Kings|Kings]]'' 6:25 relates an account of a siege in [[Samaria]] in which the desperate population consumed the excrement of doves (chiriyonim). "There was a great famine in Samaria; and behold, they besieged it, until a donkey's head was sold for eighty shekels of silver, and a fourth of a kab of dove's dung for five shekels of silver". There has been considerable discussion as to the exact meaning of this term. In notes taken by [[Paul Dietrich Giseke]] from a lecture by Linnaeus{{sfn |Linnaeus |1792 |p=287}} it is suggested that this ''Stercus Columbarum'' was in fact the plant ''Ornithogalum umbellatum'', which grew abundantly in Palestine and "unde Anglis ''Bethlem's Star'' dicitur" (is known to the English as Bethlem's Star), the white colour of which resembled the excrement of birds, hence the name ''lac Avium'' (bird milk or ''Ornithogalum''), and which was still eaten by the poor of that country.{{sfn |Moldenke |Moldenke |2017 |loc=pp. 153–154}}{{sfn |Smith |1877|loc=pp. 614–615}}

Culturally, the flower has been associated with purity and hope, atonement and reconciliation.{{sfn |Flowerinfo |2019}}

